# Copy this file to apps/api/.env (the API loads its env from its own working
# directory, not the repo root) and fill in real values before running.

NODE_ENV=development

# IANA timezone the API runs in. Daily reminder + escalation crons,
# log timestamps, and Date.toLocaleString() all honour this.
# Default is Asia/Kolkata (IST, UTC+5:30). Set Europe/London, America/New_York, etc. for other regions.
TZ=Asia/Kolkata

# Defaults below assume a LOCAL, no-Docker install against XAMPP's bundled MySQL — default
# port 3306, user root, EMPTY password. If instead you're running `docker compose up` (see
# README "Deploy"), that MySQL container is exposed on host port 3307 with user root / password
# "password" — swap every "localhost:3306" below for "localhost:3307:password@..." accordingly.
# Getting this pairing backwards (Docker's port/password against a local server, or vice versa)
# is the #1 cause of a boot-time "Authentication failed against database server" error.
DATABASE_URL="mysql://root:@localhost:3306/timesheet_portal"
# The platform's own database — org registry, SSO config, plan tiers, platform-admin accounts.
# Separate from DATABASE_URL (which, once multi-tenancy is wired up, becomes one tenant's own
# database rather than "the" database). A single self-hosted/on-prem deployment still needs
# this set, even though it'll only ever have one Organization row.
CONTROL_DATABASE_URL="mysql://root:@localhost:3306/timesphere_control"
# The Organization.slug served when a request's Host header carries no real tenant subdomain
# (localhost, a bare IP, an on-prem domain without subdomain routing). The seed creates an org
# with this slug, so a single-org deployment can leave it alone. Only change it if you renamed
# that org's slug — a mismatch makes every request 404 with "organization not found".
DEFAULT_ORG_SLUG="default"
# The MySQL server new tenant databases get physically created on when a platform admin
# provisions an org through the /platform-admin console (Phase B8). A DSN with credentials but
# NO database name. Leave unset to disable in-app provisioning (provision tenant databases some
# other way and register them directly via OrgDatabase).
TENANT_DB_PROVISION_BASE_URL="mysql://root:@localhost:3306"
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET="replace-with-strong-access-secret"
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET="replace-with-strong-refresh-secret"
# Deliberately separate from the two secrets above — signs platform-admin tokens only
# (Phase B6's /platform-admin console), so a leaked tenant secret can't be used to mint
# access to the platform-wide admin console.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_JWT_SECRET="replace-with-strong-platform-admin-secret"
# 32 random bytes, hex-encoded (64 hex chars) — encrypts IMAP passwords and BYOK LLM API
# keys at rest. Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
ENCRYPTION_KEY="replace-with-64-hex-chars-from-openssl-rand--hex-32"
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL="15m"
REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=14
API_PORT=4000
# Comma-separated CORS allow-list. In development, any private LAN IP
# (10.x.x.x / 192.168.x.x / 172.16-31.x.x / localhost) is also accepted
# automatically so testing from a phone / second machine just works.
# In production: pin to your real domain(s) and set NODE_ENV=production.
#
# REACHING THE APP OVER A PUBLIC IP OR DOMAIN? Add that exact origin here — scheme, host and port,
# no trailing slash, e.g. "https://203.0.113.10:5173". A public address is deliberately NEVER
# auto-allowed, not even in development: the private-LAN shortcut above works because those ranges
# cannot be reached from the internet, and a pattern loose enough to match one public address is
# loose enough to match an attacker's. List both http:// and https:// if you switch between them —
# the web dev server serves HTTPS when apps/web/certs exists and plain HTTP when it does not, and
# the two are different origins to a browser.
#
# A browser will report the refusal as "Origin ... not allowed by CORS" on sign-in. The origin in
# that message is the exact string to paste here.
WEB_ORIGIN="http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173"
# Every link the app EMAILS (password reset, welcome, digests) is built on this base. localhost
# only opens on the machine running the server.
#
# IF PEOPLE REACH THE APP OVER A PUBLIC IP OR DOMAIN, SET THIS TO THAT ADDRESS. "auto" detects the
# machine's own LAN IPv4, so every emailed link points at a private address that nobody outside the
# network can open — the app works for them and its emails do not. There is only one base and it is
# baked into each message at send time, so this is a choice between audiences: pick the address the
# people who RECEIVE mail can reach. (On a network without NAT hairpinning, internal users may not be
# able to open a public address from inside — test one reset link both ways before settling.)
#
# LEAVE IT ON "auto" FOR ANY MACHINE THIS CHECKOUT GETS COPIED TO. A hardcoded address travels with
# the code and is wrong everywhere except the box it was written on; "auto" resolves to whatever LAN
# address the machine actually has, at boot, and logs what it picked. CORS auto-accepts private LAN
# origins in development, so a fresh box needs no edit to either setting.
#
# Three forms:
#   "auto"                      — detect this machine's LAN IPv4 at boot; port 5173; https
#                                 exactly when apps/web/certs exist (the vite signal). The right
#                                 choice for LAN demos: survives DHCP address changes (restart
#                                 the API after one; the boot log prints what it resolved to).
#   "https://{lan-ip}:8443"     — same detection, your own scheme/port.
#   "https://timesphere.co.example" — pin a real domain; what production should do.
APP_BASE_URL="auto"

# SMTP — leave SMTP_HOST empty to fall back to console logging in development.
#
# Deliverability note: MAIL_FROM should be an address on the SAME domain as the SMTP account —
# sending "from" your corporate domain through a personal Gmail is exactly what recipient spam
# filters flag, and corporate (Microsoft 365) recipients often silently quarantine it.
#
# Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online (e.g. a @yourcompany.com mailbox):
#   SMTP_HOST="smtp.office365.com"
#   SMTP_PORT=587
#   SMTP_SECURE=false            # STARTTLS on 587, not implicit TLS
#   SMTP_USER="no-reply@yourcompany.com"
#   SMTP_PASS="<app password>"   # requires MFA on the account + "Authenticated SMTP" enabled
#                                # for the mailbox (Exchange admin → mailbox → Email apps).
#   MAIL_FROM="TimeSphere <no-reply@yourcompany.com>"  # MUST be the authenticated mailbox (or
#                                # one it has Send-As rights on) — Exchange refuses others.
# If verification fails with "535 5.7.139 ... SmtpClientAuthentication is disabled for the
# Tenant": SMTP AUTH is off org-wide. The PER-MAILBOX toggle overrides it — M365 admin center →
# Users → the mailbox → Mail → Manage email apps → tick "Authenticated SMTP" (or PowerShell:
# Set-CASMailbox -Identity <mailbox> -SmtpClientAuthenticationDisabled $false), wait up to an
# hour. Security Defaults / Conditional Access "block legacy auth" policies also block this.
# Microsoft is retiring basic-auth SMTP submission; if app passwords are unavailable in your
# tenant, use a relay (Brevo/SendGrid/SES/Azure Communication Services) with these same four
# fields — domain verification (SPF+DKIM) there also fixes deliverability properly.
MAIL_FROM="Timesheet Portal <no-reply@timesheet.local>"
SMTP_HOST=""
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=""
SMTP_PASS=""
SMTP_SECURE=false

# Uploads + SLA
UPLOAD_DIR=uploads

# ── File storage: where uploaded files actually live ─────────────────────────────────────────
# UPLOAD_DIR above is RELATIVE by default, which means every uploaded file sits inside the repo
# working tree, where a `git checkout`, `git clean` or a redeploy that replaces the directory
# destroys it. The four variables below move storage onto its own volume and segregate it into
# three subtrees an admin can back up (and audit) separately.
#
# LEAVE THEM ALL EMPTY AND NOTHING CHANGES. Empty means "keep today's layout exactly":
#   <UPLOAD_DIR>/                 documents (ticket/timesheet + email-intake attachments)
#   <UPLOAD_DIR>/avatars/         profile pictures, now per user: avatars/<userId>/<file>
#   <UPLOAD_DIR>/face/            face (biometric) imagery, already <orgId>/<userId>/<file>
#
# Setting STORAGE_ROOT alone moves all three together, keeping that shape:
#   STORAGE_ROOT=C:\TimeSphere_Uploads       →  C:\TimeSphere_Uploads\{,avatars\,face\}
#   STORAGE_ROOT=/srv/timesphere/uploads     →  /srv/timesphere/uploads/{,avatars/,face/}
# The three *_DIR overrides pin one subtree somewhere else entirely — e.g. face imagery on an
# encrypted volume, or documents on a NAS/UNC share while avatars stay local.
#
# RULES, enforced at boot with a named error (see apps/api/src/config/env.ts):
#   - must be ABSOLUTE (a relative path resolves against whatever directory the service starts
#     in, which differs between `npm run dev`, `node dist/...`, systemd and Docker);
#   - no ".." segments;
#   - the directory must exist and be writable by the service account — create it and grant
#     access BEFORE restarting. Workspace Settings → Storage & logs validates a candidate path
#     for you and shows the live writability of each resolved directory.
#
# CHANGING A PATH AFFECTS NEW FILES ONLY. Nothing is moved or deleted for you. Reads of existing
# documents fall back to the previous root automatically, so relocating never 404s old
# attachments; to finish the move, copy the old tree across yourself while the API is stopped.
STORAGE_ROOT=""
STORAGE_DOCUMENTS_DIR=""
STORAGE_AVATARS_DIR=""
STORAGE_FACE_DIR=""

# ── Rotating file logs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EMPTY LOG_DIR = OFF (the default): console only, exactly as before. Set an absolute directory
# to additionally mirror everything the process prints into rotating files. Console/stdout output
# is never taken away — Docker, `npm run dev` and systemd journals all still work.
#   LOG_DIR=C:\TimeSphere_Logs      or      LOG_DIR=/var/log/timesphere
#
# Layout:  <LOG_DIR>/2026-08-07/app-2026-08-07_00-04.log     ← 00:00–03:59 local, current
#          <LOG_DIR>/2026-08-06/app-2026-08-06_20-24.log.gz  ← previous day, gzipped on rollover
# One directory per calendar date, LOG_ROTATE_HOURS-sized files inside it. The date is repeated
# in the filename so a file copied out of its directory still says which day it is.
#
# On the first write after midnight the previous day's files are gzipped, then day-directories
# older than LOG_RETENTION_DAYS are deleted. A process restarted after being down overnight does
# the same catch-up at boot, so retention is honoured even if it never observes a rollover.
# An unwritable LOG_DIR degrades to console-only with one warning; it never stops the app.
#
# Left EMPTY here on purpose. This file is copied verbatim to apps/api/.env by `npm run setup`,
# and an absolute path is validated against the platform the API actually boots on — a Windows
# path shipped as the default fails Zod on Linux/macOS and stops a fresh clone booting at all.
LOG_DIR=""
LOG_ROTATE_HOURS=4
LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30
LOG_COMPRESS_ON_ROLLOVER=true

SLA_DEFAULT_APPROVAL_HOURS=48
SLA_CRON_SCHEDULE="*/15 * * * *"
SLA_ENABLED=true

# Ticket SLA — hours to resolve a ticket before it escalates, by priority
TICKET_SLA_ENABLED=true
TICKET_SLA_CRON_SCHEDULE="*/15 * * * *"
TICKET_SLA_LOW_HOURS=168
TICKET_SLA_MEDIUM_HOURS=72
TICKET_SLA_HIGH_HOURS=24
TICKET_SLA_CRITICAL_HOURS=4

# AI features (auto-triage, duplicate detection, writing assistant + AI refine, email intake).
# Leave empty to keep every AI feature inert regardless of the GlobalAISettings toggles.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# MCP server (TimeSphere AS an MCP server, POST /api/mcp) — deliberately has NO variable here.
# Whether it is on, whether writes are allowed, which tools are live and who each credential acts
# as all live in the tenant database (GlobalMcpSettings / McpCredential) and are edited at runtime
# from Workspace Settings -> MCP server. It ships switched off, so there is nothing to configure
# to keep it off. The per-user AI throttle (20 req/min) is likewise fixed in code, not tunable.
# See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md "Operating the MCP server".

# Update checks + the What's-new release history (all optional).
# UPDATE_CHECK=off disables the hourly GitHub call entirely (air-gapped posture); the What's-new
# page then shows the release history bundled inside this build's CHANGELOG.md instead.
# UPDATE_CHECK_REPO overrides the owner/repo for forks.
# UPDATE_CHECK_TOKEN: fine-grained PAT with read-only Contents — needed only when the repo is
# PRIVATE (anonymous release lookups 404 there). Without it the page still shows bundled history.
# UPDATE_CHECK=on
# UPDATE_CHECK_REPO="adityapuppala1/TimeSheet"
# UPDATE_CHECK_TOKEN=""

# Reverse proxies in front of this API. EVERY per-IP rate limit in the app (the 20/min login
# limiter above all) reads req.ip, and Express only derives that from X-Forwarded-For when this
# is set. Behind a proxy at 0, req.ip is the PROXY's address for every caller, so those limiters
# silently become one shared global bucket and a single attacker can exhaust the login limit for
# everyone.
#
# It is a HOP COUNT, not a boolean, on purpose: trusting X-Forwarded-For wholesale lets anyone
# forge req.ip. Set it to the real number of hops — nginx alone = 1, Cloudflare in front of
# nginx = 2 — and leave it at 0 when the container is exposed directly (including npm run dev).
# TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1

# Blanket per-IP request budget (requests/minute, default 900 ≈ 15 req/s). Raise it when many
# real users share ONE egress IP (office NAT, corporate proxy) — a 9am login rush across a
# hundred people behind one IP easily exceeds 900/min and they all start seeing 429s. The
# stricter per-surface limiters (auth 20/min, public share links, webhooks) are NOT affected.
# RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=900

# Connections per tenant Prisma client (default 5 — multi-tenant arithmetic: 50 cached tenant
# clients x 5 stays under MySQL max_connections=151). Single-org deployments should raise it:
# load-tested at 50 concurrent users, 5 connections capped the authed path near 90 req/s from
# pool queueing alone; 20 removed the queue. The compose files and Helm chart ship 20.
# TENANT_DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT=5

# API request telemetry — per-request timings behind Workspace Settings → Maintenance →
# "API performance" (latency percentiles, slowest endpoints, per-host/pod split, request log).
#
# OFF BY DEFAULT because this middleware sits in the hot path of every request and writes a row
# per sampled one. On a busy deployment turn the RATE down rather than the feature off —
# percentiles from a 10% sample are still percentiles, whereas no data answers nothing.
# API_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true
# API_TELEMETRY_SAMPLE_RATE=1          # 0..1 — fraction of requests recorded
# API_TELEMETRY_FLUSH_MS=5000          # how often the in-memory buffer drains to the database
# API_TELEMETRY_MAX_BUFFER=5000        # past this, samples are dropped (and counted) not queued
# API_TELEMETRY_RETENTION_DAYS=14      # nightly prune at 04:10; these rows accumulate with traffic
#
# Host identity stamped on each row. Named for the Kubernetes downward API so a Deployment can map
# them straight through (fieldRef: metadata.name / metadata.namespace). Off-cluster, leave them
# unset — the columns are written NULL rather than filled with a guess, and the hostname still
# comes from os.hostname().
# POD_NAME=""
# POD_NAMESPACE=""
# CLUSTER_NAME=""
